{
  "case_id": "2006-05-1629",
  "document_hash": "779ef5be0b12641cca62a8a9b6cc1946c6435ee77b60cd6adba724dcb0b84a51",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Dan Himmelfarb)",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1629",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "779ef5be0b12641cca62a8a9b6cc1946c6435ee77b60cd6adba724dcb0b84a51",
      "evidence_id": "ev-779ef5be0b12-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Ninth Circuit held that the term theft offense, an aggravated felony under the Immigration and Nationality Act, does not include aiding and abetting. That holding is incorrect. Indeed, it is so clearly incorrect that even respondent does not defend it."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1629",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "779ef5be0b12641cca62a8a9b6cc1946c6435ee77b60cd6adba724dcb0b84a51",
      "evidence_id": "ev-779ef5be0b12-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Will you help me out on one mechanical point? As you probably know from your brief, I don't come from a jurisdiction that uses this rule and I'm just not used to it. I had thought -- and I guess I'm wrong -- that if the natural and probable consequences theory were used to prove, let's say, ultimately the offense of assault, in what started out as a theft case, that there would have to be a separate charge of assault but that the theory of proof would be the natural and consequences extension of aiding and abetting so that there would at least be on the record a charge of assault."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1629",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "779ef5be0b12641cca62a8a9b6cc1946c6435ee77b60cd6adba724dcb0b84a51",
      "evidence_id": "ev-779ef5be0b12-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Can he be convicted of -- of the consequential crime if he is never charged with the consequential crime? You charge him with the -- with the theft and convict him of assault?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1629",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "779ef5be0b12641cca62a8a9b6cc1946c6435ee77b60cd6adba724dcb0b84a51",
      "evidence_id": "ev-779ef5be0b12-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Himmelfarb, does the government urge that we consider the point that you're now arguing and the other points? You started out by saying everyone agrees that the rationale of the Ninth Circuit won't wash, but if we go beyond that, then we are deciding the question as a matter of first view instead of review."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1629",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "779ef5be0b12641cca62a8a9b6cc1946c6435ee77b60cd6adba724dcb0b84a51",
      "evidence_id": "ev-779ef5be0b12-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. HIMMELFARB: That's right. I mean, our submission is that Congress's intent in enacting an aggravated felony provision that captures aiders and abettors was that minor variations in formulation wouldn't matter for the reasons I'm giving. So it's ultimately a matter of Congressional intent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1629",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "779ef5be0b12641cca62a8a9b6cc1946c6435ee77b60cd6adba724dcb0b84a51",
      "evidence_id": "ev-779ef5be0b12-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Is it the sentence given or the sentence prescribed for the crime?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1629",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "779ef5be0b12641cca62a8a9b6cc1946c6435ee77b60cd6adba724dcb0b84a51",
      "evidence_id": "ev-779ef5be0b12-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: But what do we do about that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Meade",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1629",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "779ef5be0b12641cca62a8a9b6cc1946c6435ee77b60cd6adba724dcb0b84a51",
      "evidence_id": "ev-779ef5be0b12-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the Government gets two bites at the apple here. They get a first bite on the categoric approach where all they need to show is that all the elements are within the generic definition of the crime."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Meade",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1629",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "779ef5be0b12641cca62a8a9b6cc1946c6435ee77b60cd6adba724dcb0b84a51",
      "evidence_id": "ev-779ef5be0b12-0002-0003",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The natural and probable consequences theory cuts across a wide variety of crimes, as the government points out."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1629",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "779ef5be0b12641cca62a8a9b6cc1946c6435ee77b60cd6adba724dcb0b84a51",
      "evidence_id": "ev-779ef5be0b12-0002-0002",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 50,
        "start": 50
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "REBUTTAL ARGUMENT OF DAN HIMMELFARB ON BEHALF OF PETITIONER"
